From 392946303ce7bbf5cf4bd32dddaea912da6fe979 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shameer Kolothum <skolothumtho@nvidia.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 09:35:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] pci/pcie: Do not check Link Status 2 across a migration

pcie_sync_bridge_lnk() copies the Flit Mode Status bit of the device below
a bridge into the Link Status 2 register of the bridge itself.

When the device below the bridge is assigned with vfio-pci, config_read()
returns the value of the real hardware. Two cards can easily disagree on
it, even two of the same model in the same machine, because it depends on
how each link trained. The bit then ends up set on one side of a migration
and clear on the other. This does not need two hosts. It reproduces with
both ends running on one machine and a different card assigned to each.

Any VM with an assigned device below a pcie-root-port is affected.

Signed-off-by: Shameer Kolothum <skolothumtho@nvidia.com>
---
 hw/pci/pcie.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/pci/pcie.c b/hw/pci/pcie.c
index 4622c75e48..054ca03c92 100644
--- a/hw/pci/pcie.c
+++ b/hw/pci/pcie.c
@@ -244,6 +244,15 @@ int pcie_cap_init(PCIDevice *dev, uint8_t offset,
     /* Fill link speed and width options */
     pcie_cap_fill_slot_lnk(dev);
 
+    /*
+     * Link Status 2 carries the Flit Mode Status bit, which
+     * pcie_sync_bridge_lnk() copies from the device below the bridge. For an
+     * assigned device that value comes from real hardware and can differ
+     * between two hosts, so checking it on migration would reject an
+     * otherwise valid stream. Same reasoning as PCI_EXP_LNKSTA above.
+     */
+    pci_set_word(dev->cmask + pos + PCI_EXP_LNKSTA2, 0);
+
     /* Filling v2 specific values */
     pci_set_long(exp_cap + PCI_EXP_DEVCAP2,
                  PCI_EXP_DEVCAP2_EFF | PCI_EXP_DEVCAP2_EETLPP);
-- 
